---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import ReimbursementCalculator from '../components/ReimbursementCalculator.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<Layout 
    title="Business Mileage Reimbursement Calculator 2026"
    description="Calculate your business mileage reimbursement using the standard IRS rate for 2026. Perfect for independent contractors, freelancers, and employees."
    keywords="mileage reimbursement calculator, business mileage calculator, irs mileage rate 2026, driving mileage calculator, standard mileage deduction"
>
    <h1>Mileage Reimbursement Calculator</h1>
    
    <ReimbursementCalculator />

    <div class="seo-content glass-card">
        <h2>Calculate Your Driving Mileage Reimbursement</h2>
        <p>If you drive your personal vehicle for business purposes, you are likely entitled to a <strong>mileage calculator reimbursement</strong>. Our built-in reimbursement tool lets you quickly multiply your business miles by the standard rate (or your company's custom rate) to generate your exact expense report amount.</p>
        
        <h3>The Standard IRS Mileage Rate</h3>
        <p>The IRS sets a standard mileage rate each year to calculate the deductible costs of operating an automobile for business, charitable, medical, or moving purposes. For 2024, the rate was $0.67 per mile, with expected updates for 2025 and 2026. This rate accounts for fuel, depreciation, insurance, and maintenance costs.</p>
        
        <h3>How to Calculate Reimbursement for Driving</h3>
        <p>To calculate your <strong>driving mileage calculator</strong> reimbursement, simply record the total miles driven exclusively for business purposes. Then, multiply those miles by the IRS standard mileage rate or your employer's specific rate. For example, 100 miles × $0.67 = $67.00 reimbursement.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What counts as business mileage?</h3>
            <p>Business mileage includes driving from your office to a client meeting, driving between multiple job sites, or running business errands. Commuting from your home to your regular workplace is generally <strong>not</strong> considered deductible business mileage.</p>
        </div>
        
        <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Do I need to keep a mileage log?</h3>
            <p>Yes. The IRS requires you to keep a detailed log of your business mileage. Your log must include the date, destination, business purpose, and the starting and ending odometer readings for each trip.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</Layout>
